Hello OMG! Ubuntu team,

an app tip for the Linux desktop, with the details your readers usually ask
for first.

Solidon3D edits downloaded 3D models without CAD knowledge. Click a hole in a
printable part and its dimensions appear on the model itself, type 8 instead
of 6 and the countersink follows, pick a fit for the screw, read the check
report, hand the file to the slicer you already use. It ships as a Flatpak and
an AppImage for Linux, alongside Windows and macOS builds. One-time purchase,
no subscription. After a single activation it runs fully offline: no account,
no telemetry, no server requirement. A machine with no network connection can
be activated via a file and a second device.

Version 0.5.0 is a large step. Until now an edited model came back out as a
triangle mesh; now it is an exact body with real faces and edges, and holes,
countersinks and threads stay exact when they are moved, duplicated or
removed. STEP goes in and comes back out cleanly, so the result can be carried
into FreeCAD or any other CAD tool.

That is also why the program exists. FreeCAD and Fusion accept an STL but
their tools do not grip triangles, and the mesh tools that could edit them are
discontinued. Adapting a downloaded part so it fits something has been without
a proper tool for years.

The most immediately visible change: a honeycomb pattern, a knurl or a set of
ribs is recognised as one feature with pitch, cell width and depth instead of
hundreds of faces, and can be removed with a click or reapplied with three
numbers, including around a cylindrical handle.

There is an assistant with AI features. It is optional, can be switched off
entirely, and can run locally on your own GPU, so nothing is required to leave
the machine.

Interface and manual are in six languages. The demo is free and complete until
the end of October, so there is nothing to request from me to try it.

